## Phase 3: Material Synthesis (The High-Heat Clay Kiln)
To transition from weak, sun-baked clay to vitrified, waterproof ceramics, you must construct a specialized, oxygen-throttled furnace. [8, 9] 
## 🛠️ The Crossdraft Kiln Blueprint
A standard open fire tops out around 600°C, which is insufficient for metallurgy or strong pottery. A crossdraft kiln solves this by pulling air horizontally over fuel and radiating the trapped heat across your materials. [7, 8, 10] 

                [ Chimney ]  <-- Draws air upward, pulling heat through

                |         |
                |___  |
 [ Firebox ] -> [Pottery] |  <-- Firing Chamber (Traps thermal mass)
 [  Grate  ]    [Chamber] |


   1. The Base & Grate: Dig a 30 cm horizontal trench into a clay hillside. Lay a clay-perforated shelf (the grate) halfway up the trench to separate your burning charcoal from the ash falling below.
   2. The Firing Chamber: Build a dome over the back half of the trench using a mix of mud, wild grass (for binding), and grog (crushed, pre-burnt pottery bits that prevent structural cracking).
   3. The Chimney: Construct a vertical clay pipe (at least 1 meter tall) at the very rear of the dome. The rising hot air in the chimney creates a low-pressure vacuum, aggressively sucking fresh oxygen through the front firebox and elevating chamber temperatures past 1000°C. [9, 10] 

