## Phase 4: The Metallurgical Revolution (Mining & Smelting)
With your kiln operational, you can step out of the Stone Age by locating and reducing metallic oxides. [1, 8] 
## ⛏️ How to Identify Metal Ores in the Wild
Look along geological fault lines, exposed cliffsides, and riverbeds for heavily weathered, discolored mineral veins: [1, 11] 

| Metal | Primary Ore Target | Visual/Physical Clues |
|---|---|---|
| Copper | Malachite / Azurite | Bright, vibrant neon-green or deep-blue crusts running through rock faces. |
| Iron | Hematite / Magnetite | Dark red, rusty brown, or mustard-yellow rock layers. Incredibly heavy and magnetic. |
| Tin | Cassiterite | Heavy, dull black or dark brown bulbous pebbles settled in river gravel beds. |

## 🫗 The Copper Smelting Process

   1. Ore Processing: Crush your green malachite stones into a fine, sand-like powder using a heavy stone mortar and pestle.
   2. The Charge: Mix 1 part crushed ore directly with 2 parts crushed charcoal. The charcoal acts as both your high-temperature fuel and a chemical reducing agent.
   3. The Reduction: Pack this mixture inside your high-heat kiln. Use a hollow clay reed (a tuyere) to blow a continuous stream of air directly into the base of the fire.
   4. Chemical Change: At roughly 1,083°C, the carbon in the burning charcoal strips away the oxygen atoms from the copper oxide (releasing carbon dioxide gas). Pure, molten copper drops to the bottom of your kiln furnace as glowing red liquid nodules, ready to be poured into charcoal molds. [1, 8, 12] 

